  "generated_text": "First, we rewrite the given equation:\n\\[56x^2 + 27 = 89x - 8.\\]\n\nNext, we move all terms to one side of the equation to set it to zero:\n\\[56x^2 - 89x + 35 = 0.\\]\n\nWe know that \\(x = \\frac{5}{7}\\) is a solution. To find the other solution, we can use the fact that the quadratic equation \\(56x^2 - 89x + 35 = 0\\) has roots that sum to \\(\\frac{89}{56}\\) and multiply to \\(\\frac{35}{56}\\).\n\nLet \\(r\\) be the other root. Then:\n\\[\\frac{5}{7} + r = \\frac{89}{56},\\]\n\\[r = \\frac{89}{56} - \\frac{5}{7} = \\frac{24}{56} = \\frac{3}{7}.\\]\n\nThus, the other value of \\(x\\) that solves the equation is:\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{3}{7}}.\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[3/7, '\\\\frac{3}{7}']",
    "gold_answer": "First of all, let us place everything to one side: \\begin{align*}\n56x^2 + 27 &= 89x - 8\\\\\n56x^2 - 89x + 35 &= 0.\n\\end{align*}Now we must factor. Knowing that $x = \\frac{5}{7}$ is a solution to this equation, we can reason that $(7x - 5)$ must be one of the factors of $56x^2 - 89x + 35$ which means that $(8x - 7)$ must be the other factor, since the linear terms must multiply to $56x^2$ and the constant terms must multiply to $35.$\n\nWe can easily verify that indeed, $56x^2 - 89x + 35 = (7x - 5)(8x - 7),$ therefore $x = \\boxed{\\frac{7}{8}}$ is our answer.",
    "is_correct": false
  },
